  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Upper level low pressure will drift northeast \nas weak ridging brings a gradual warmup and diminishing rain\nchances Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nAn upper trough continues to be in place over the northeast US,\nand a modestly strong shortwave rotating around its base has led\nto a cloudy, cool, and showery day, especially across northern\nPA. Bradford's high of 58F was the coolest since May 24th and\n6th earliest such occurrence on record (start date August 1). \n\nNorthwest flow aloft will continue to generate clouds and\noccasional showers across the higher elevations of northern and\nwestern PA tonight into Tuesday. By later Tuesday, upper\ntroughing will give way to some upper ridging with improving\nconditions, clearing skies, and a trend toward warmer\ntemperatures. \n\nHighs on Tuesday will still be a few degrees below normal, but\npush 5 to 10 degrees warmer than Monday. The higher elevations\ncould stay in the upper 60s while the lower Susquehanna likely\ncrests the 80F mark. Clear skies and lighter winds Tuesday night\nlikely lead to fairly widespread fog formation into Wednesday\nmorning. Wednesday trends warmer with even more sunshine and\nhighs in the mid 70s to mid 80s.\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers and thunderstorms return Wed night or \nThursday as a cold front approaches from the west.\n\nReturn flow around the high will bring more moisture up through\nthe Mid-Atlantic and into the Susq Valley late Wed night. This\nwill touch off a few showers. A cold front will be approaching\nfrom the west at the same time. Diurnal heating will combine\nwith upper energy to kick off numerous SHRA/TSRA on Thursday.\nThe front probably won't fully pass thru the CWA until Friday\nmorning. So, Thursday night (Lunar Eclipse time) probably won't\nbe clearing enough for much of the CWA to be able to see it\nwell. The west has the best chance to do so, but confidence is\nlow in sky cover at this range.",
